## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical risk management when relying on external data and requires a concrete rollout plan across regions with resilience patterns and measurable outcomes.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and canary deployments
- SLOs and data freshness metrics
- Resilience patterns: circuit breakers, fallback caches
- Rollout artifacts, runbooks, and incident drills

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight circuit-breaker with fallback
class Breaker {
  constructor(threshold=3) { this.failures=0; this.open=false; }
  async call(fn) {
    if (this.open) throw new Error('Circuit open');
    try { return await fn(); } catch (e) { this.failures++; if (this.failures >= threshold) this.open=true; throw e; }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate ETA accuracy during canary without impacting users?
- How would you design post-incident reviews and remedial actions?

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates governance design capabilities for small remote teams, focusing on beginner-friendly processes that balance innovation with stability; emphasizes risk management, stakeholder communication, and scalable automation.

## Key Concepts
- Semantic versioning methodology
- Deprecation policy frameworks
- Automated compatibility testing
- Feature flag rollbacks
- Partner communication protocols

## Code Example
```javascript
// Version compatibility validator
function isCompatible(currentVersion, targetVersion) {
  const [cMA, cMi, cPa] = currentVersion.split('.').map(Number);
  const [tMA, tMi, tPa] = targetVersion.split('.').map(Number);

  // Major version changes are breaking
  if (cMA !== tMA) return false;

  // Minor version changes are backward compatible
  if (cMi > tMi) return false;

  return true;
}
```
